{
  "image_name": "71249383039b6f648af4ab97ac282f49.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways of combining a driving field with a delayed gate field to generate a short, sub-cycle charge burst in a device sketched at the top right of each panel. In both cases, a blue waveform labeled as the source field and a red waveform representing a delayed gate field are plotted along a time axis, with arrows indicating the relative delay between them.\n\nThe key difference is the shape of the gate field. In the “streaking” case, the red field is a mostly smooth ramp or low-frequency transient that overlaps the blue oscillations and appears to steer or time-shift the resulting charge motion, producing a localized burst near the overlap region. In the “perturbative” case, the delayed red field is itself strongly oscillatory, so the interaction looks more like two wave packets crossing, again creating a brief burst when they coincide.\n\nThe device drawing suggests a nanogap or junction between electrodes connected to an ammeter, where the combined fields drive current. Overall, the image emphasizes that varying the temporal character of the delayed gate field changes the mechanism of control, while both schemes use timing between two fields to confine charge transfer to a sub-cycle timescale.",
